Good stuff. Assuming the backtrace is accurate, the issue is here:
~~~~
    gd = xcalloc(1, sizeof *gd);
	gd->sx = sx;
	gd->sy = sy;
...
	if (gd->sy != 0)
		gd->linedata = xcalloc(gd->sy, sizeof *gd->linedata);

	grid_check_is_clear(gd);
~~~~
Which means that there is nothing in tmux that can be causing this between the allocation and check. Apple's `calloc` seems to be returning an allocation that is not fully zeroed.

This could be a bug in the allocator or it could be that it returns pages it assumes are zero but that have been accidentally overwritten.

TBH neither of these seem very likely. If there was a bug of this magnitude in macOS libc one would think it would be obvious. But equally, if tmux is scribbling over areas of unallocated heap, why is it crashing so consistently in one place? And why only ever on macOS ARM?

I am a bit out of my depth, but I set an AI on it. For the larger bug, I didn't get anything conclusive about the ultimate cause, but the mechanism it found seems right. The AI thinks that there is probably a write-after-free *somewhere* in tmux. According to https://manp.gs/mac/3/malloc#MallocZeroOnFree, `free` zeroes memory, and `calloc` is allowed to return non-zero memory if something wrote to this freed memory. This is configurable: there's an environment variable that causes calloc to actually zero memory, and suppresses the zero-on-free, but Apple discourages using it. (There are also some environment variables for debugging modes, but they only work on non-ASAN builds)

But for the above bug, https://github.com/tmux/tmux/issues/5385#issuecomment-4997918988, it has a fix (on top of ed4245a):

```diff
diff --git a/server-client.c b/server-client.c
index 3fb7813ca4..54631818bf 100644
--- a/server-client.c
+++ b/server-client.c
@@ -541,6 +541,7 @@

 	free(c->title);
 	free((void *)c->cwd);
+	c->cwd = NULL;

 	evtimer_del(&c->repeat_timer);
 	evtimer_del(&c->click_timer);
@@ -2885,7 +2886,7 @@
 {
 	const char	*home;

-	if (!cfg_finished && cfg_client != NULL)
+	if (!cfg_finished && cfg_client != NULL && cfg_client->cwd != NULL)
 		return (cfg_client->cwd);
 	if (c != NULL && c->session == NULL && c->cwd != NULL)
 		return (c->cwd);
```

I'm not posting this as a PR because I didn't fully trace the bug, I'm hoping it'll be easy for a more experienced person to verify the fix.

Unfortunately, this is a different bug (it's a read-after-free, not a write-after-free like the `grid_check_is_clear` bug); it's unclear whether there's any relationship (could *something like* the latter bug cause a write-after-free?)

Thanks. This ASAN report is an issue but I think it is unlikely to be the actual problem here because we are not writing through the pointer, so we cannot be corrupting a freed block. `cfg_client` should be being cleared when configuration is done, it should not be left dangling like this. Can you see if you see this again with this patch? I will also apply it to `master` and it will be there in a few hours.
~~~~
--- cfg.c       14 Jul 2026 17:17:17 -0000      1.90
+++ cfg.c       17 Jul 2026 07:43:18 -0000
@@ -51,6 +51,7 @@ cfg_done(__unused struct cmdq_item *item
        if (cfg_finished)
                return (CMD_RETURN_NORMAL);
        cfg_finished = 1;
+       cfg_client = NULL;

        cfg_show_causes(NULL);

~~~~
I don't really need to see any more reports without ASAN at this point, as a general rule if you have the same backtrace I have already seen and have made changes to test based on it, there is no need to post it again.

Here is the correct fix for this ASAN issue, will be in `master` later on:
~~~~
--- server-client.c     15 Jul 2026 14:14:50 -0000      1.494
+++ server-client.c     17 Jul 2026 08:12:20 -0000
@@ -502,6 +502,8 @@ server_client_lost(struct client *c)
        struct client_file      *cf, *cf1;
        struct client_window    *cw, *cw1;

+       if (cfg_client == c)
+               cfg_client = NULL;
        c->flags |= CLIENT_DEAD;

        server_client_clear_overlay(c);
